{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>Also from yesterday, also from the day before, also from when You spoke to Your servant</b> — we have learned that all seven days the Holy One, blessed be He, sat and persuaded Moses to go on His mission. \"From yesterday and from the day before and from when You spoke\" — that is three; and three of them have the particle גַּם (also) prefixed, making six; and he was standing on the seventh day. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 4:10).\n\nAnd according to the plain sense, he says: I am heavy of mouth <b>also from yesterday also from the day before</b>, for from my youth I was heavy of mouth, all the more so now that I am old. And also <b>from when You spoke</b> today <b>to Your servant</b>, for You did not remove the heaviness of my mouth when You commanded me to go to Pharaoh to speak in Your name — and if so, how shall I go before him? Now, Moses, out of his great desire not to go, did not pray before Him, blessed be He, that He remove the heaviness of his mouth. Instead he argued: since You did not remove the heaviness of my mouth from the time You spoke to me to go, do not command me to go, for it is not fitting for the Master of all to send a messenger with uncircumcised lips to the king of the nations. And the Holy One, blessed be He — since he did not pray about this — did not wish to heal him, but said to him, \"I will be with your mouth and teach you what you shall speak\" (verse 12), meaning that My words, which I will place in your mouth, will be in precise terms that you will be able to express well.\n\nAnd in Shemot Rabbah (3:15) they said: He said to him, if you are not a man of words, do not be concerned — did I not create all the mouths in the world? And I made mute whomever I wished, and deaf, and blind, and seeing for sight, and seeing for hearing, and if I had wished you to be a man of words, you would have been; but this is what I desire, and when you speak your words will be correct, for I will be with your mouth. As it is written (Exodus 4:12), \"And now go, and I will be with your mouth.\"\n\nAnd according to this, it appears to me that He did not wish to remove from him the heaviness of mouth because it contained an element of the miracle that our Rabbis recounted (Shemot Rabbah 1:26) — what happened to him with Pharaoh.\n\nAnd the correct interpretation in my eyes is that HaShem said to Moses: who placed a mouth for man, or who makes mute — is it not I, HaShem, who makes all? And it is in My hand to heal you. And now, since you do not want your healing and did not pray before Me concerning this, go to where I command you, and I will be with your mouth and make My mission succeed.\n\nIt is also possible that there is a hint in \"And the anger of HaShem burned against Moses\" (verse 14) that He did not wish to heal him and sent him against his will."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "The phrase וְהָיָה עוֹמֵד בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי — literally 'and he was standing on the seventh day' — refers to Moses standing at the burning bush on the seventh day of the seven-day persuasion; rendered literally as Rashi's midrashic reading requires."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:02:42.050267736Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}